meta {
  name: Get Lead Context
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/quote-recaps/context?leadId={{createdLeadId}}
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  leadId: {{createdLeadId}}
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:post-response {
  // The form needs a household before it can submit; capture it so the
  // Create request below can assert the recap linked to the same one.
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setVar("quoteContextHouseholdId", res.getBody().householdId);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("returns a display-ready header", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("leadId");
    expect(body).to.have.property("primaryContactName");
    expect(body).to.have.property("householdId");
    expect(body).to.have.property("leadStatus");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /quote-recaps/context — lead + household header for the form (PAC-39)

  The read-only context `/quote-recaps/new?leadId=` shows on mount, so a producer
  can confirm they are recapping the right household before entering anything.

  - **Module:** `quote_recaps`
  - **Permission:** `quote_recaps:read`
  - **Data scope:** `own` — a lead outside the caller's scope **404s**, not 403.

  > **Depends on `Leads › Create Lead`.** That request captures `createdLeadId`
  > via `bru.setVar`. The CLI walks folders alphabetically (`Auth` → `Deal
  > Audits` → `Leads` → `Public Intake` → `Quote Recaps`), so by the time this
  > folder runs the variable exists. Running this folder on its own will 404.

  ## Query params
  | param  | type   | required | rules                     |
  |--------|--------|----------|---------------------------|
  | leadId | string | yes      | 24-char Mongo ObjectId    |

  ## Behaviour worth knowing
  **Household resolution is self-healing.** It reads `lead.householdId` first,
  then falls back to matching `lead.legacyHouseholdId` against
  `households.legacySmartSuiteId` — and backfills `lead.householdId` on the way
  through. The SmartSuite migration writes *only* the legacy string, so without
  that fallback every migrated lead would be unable to record a recap.

  **`householdId: null` is a real answer,** returned instead of an error when the
  lead has no household at all. The form uses it to block up front rather than
  letting the producer fill everything in and fail at submit; `POST
  /quote-recaps` is the backstop and returns `409` in that case.

  **`householdAddress` is normalized server-side.** Three writers have stored
  three different key shapes (`street` from intake, `line1` from the demo seed,
  `location_address` from SmartSuite); this collapses them. `null` means nothing
  usable is on file, and the form then hides the "same as household" toggle.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "leadId": "665f0a1b2c3d4e5f60718293",
    "primaryContactName": "Dana Whitfield",
    "householdId": "665f0a1b2c3d4e5f60718294",
    "householdName": "Whitfield Household",
    "householdAddress": {
      "street": "77 Oak Lane",
      "city": "Tulsa",
      "state": "OK",
      "zip": "74101"
    },
    "leadStatus": "New"
  }
  ```
  `leadStatus` is normalized — a migrated lead storing the raw code `hfwda`
  reads back as `Qualified`.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — `leadId` missing or not a 24-char id.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `quote_recaps` module disabled, or missing
    `quote_recaps:read`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such lead, or one outside the caller's data scope.
}
